1. Can We Use the FPGA's Green LED?
Looking at the FPGA top-level HDL (
ddc_sdr_top.sv
), the board's LEDs are currently hardwired in gateware as follows:

Red LED (led_red): ADC Over-the-Range indicator (pulses when ADC clips).
Yellow LED (led_yellow): SPI activity indicator (blinks whenever an SPI tuning/gain command arrives from the RP2040).
Green LED (led_green): Reset/Running indicator (assign led_green = ~internal_rst_n; — stays solid on whenever the FPGA is configured and operational).
Regarding the Pico W's on-board LED: you are completely right that writing to it requires an SPI command across the CYW43 bus. In our streaming loop, we actually turn the Pico W LED on once when streaming begins, and never touch it again while active, so it does not generate continuous SPI traffic during playback.

However, eliminating any background toggling of the CYW43 LED is still a great practice to keep that bus 100% dedicated to data.

2. How Does Switching to the Polling Method Affect the SPI Pin Sharing?
This gets to the exact heart of the problem.

In threadsafe_background mode (what we have now):
The Pico SDK configures a hardware alarm/timer interrupt that preempts the CPU behind the scenes.
Because GPIO 24 is shared between the SPI data line (MISO) and the Wi-Fi host interrupt (IRQ):
When the interrupt fires, the background ISR preempts whatever is running and tries to access the CYW43 chip over SPI.
If Core 1 is in the middle of preparing or pushing a UDP packet, or reading/writing SPI registers, the background interrupt and Core 1 fight over internal mutexes.
Worse, when GPIO 24 transitions during normal SPI data transfers, the edge-detect hardware can mistake data bits for IRQ assertions, triggering spurious background interrupts.
In pico_cyw43_arch_lwip_poll mode (the reference method):
Zero background timer interrupts or preemption. The SDK will never interrupt your code to service the network.
Core 1 has 100% deterministic control of the SPI bus in a clean, sequential loop:
c


while (true) {
    cyw43_arch_poll();        // 1. Check for incoming network packets / events
    openhpsdr_task();         // 2. Process discovery & C&C tuning packets
    // 3. Push audio samples to UDP as buffers arrive from Core 0
}
Because everything runs synchronously on Core 1:
There is zero possibility of an interrupt colliding with a streaming transfer.
The SPI bus and GPIO 24 are only ever accessed by one sequential execution thread.
Latency is completely predictable.
This is the fundamental reason why 2026_v0.2_unified runs so stably without weird deadlocks.

